1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,680
Half past two in the morning, in Asia.

2
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,944
A tiny transfer leaves
the crypto exchange Bitget.

3
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:09,184
Nobody looks at it.

4
00:00:11,684 --> 00:00:15,608
Less than three hours later,
about $388 million is gone.

5
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:19,691
And Bitget says not
a single key was stolen.

6
00:00:22,591 --> 00:00:24,311
At 19:05, the alarm went off.

7
00:00:24,311 --> 00:00:26,624
Bitget froze every customer withdrawal.

8
00:00:26,624 --> 00:00:30,380
And the money kept leaving
for another two hours.

9
00:00:30,430 --> 00:00:33,152
And the first sign
that someone was inside?

10
00:00:33,152 --> 00:00:36,685
It's in the logs. Dated 24 days earlier.

11
00:00:42,835 --> 00:00:47,465
To understand why this one hurts,
go back 19 months. February 2025.

12
00:00:47,465 --> 00:00:50,644
The exchange Bybit has
just lost $1.5 billion.

13
00:00:50,644 --> 00:00:52,509
The FBI blames North Korea.

14
00:00:52,509 --> 00:00:56,339
Customers are rushing
to pull their money out.

15
00:00:56,389 --> 00:00:58,535
That same day, a rival sends help.

16
00:00:58,535 --> 00:01:02,575
Bitget lends Bybit 40,000 ether
to keep its withdrawals running.

17
00:01:02,575 --> 00:01:04,928
No interest. No collateral.

18
00:01:04,978 --> 00:01:06,063
Remember that loan.

19
00:01:06,063 --> 00:01:08,042
It comes back at the end.

20
00:01:08,092 --> 00:01:13,331
On 24 September 2026, the exchange
that came to help became the one robbed.

21
00:01:13,331 --> 00:01:17,823
At the time, the biggest
crypto theft of the year.

22
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:23,243
I'm Thomas Archer, the AI
narrator of Siphon Files.

23
00:01:23,293 --> 00:01:27,379
Every claim in this episode has a source,
and they're all in the description.

24
00:01:27,429 --> 00:01:28,834
Here's the strange part.

25
00:01:28,834 --> 00:01:32,487
Bitget says not a single
private key was compromised.

26
00:01:32,537 --> 00:01:37,156
So how do you steal
$388 million without the keys?

27
00:01:37,206 --> 00:01:40,117
You make the exchange sign it for you.

28
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:45,601
An exchange keeps most
of its money in cold wallets.

29
00:01:45,601 --> 00:01:47,488
Offline, slow, hard to reach.

30
00:01:47,488 --> 00:01:49,469
Those weren't touched.

31
00:01:49,519 --> 00:01:51,014
Warm wallets hold a reserve.

32
00:01:51,014 --> 00:01:55,136
Hot wallets pay your withdrawals
automatically, around the clock.

33
00:01:55,186 --> 00:01:58,194
When you press withdraw,
a request goes into a pipeline.

34
00:01:58,194 --> 00:01:59,429
Risk checks look at it.

35
00:01:59,429 --> 00:02:04,329
Then a signing service puts a cryptographic
signature on it, and the money moves.

36
00:02:04,379 --> 00:02:06,423
Without that signature, nothing leaves.

37
00:02:06,423 --> 00:02:08,379
With it, anything does.

38
00:02:08,429 --> 00:02:11,255
So the attacker never needed
the secret behind the signature.

39
00:02:11,255 --> 00:02:14,460
They needed to get close
to the machine that uses it.

40
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:18,705
And for that, they needed
a way in that nobody would watch.

41
00:02:20,905 --> 00:02:27,091
31 August. That's the earliest malicious
activity investigators found in the logs.

42
00:02:27,141 --> 00:02:28,545
Not inside the wallets.

43
00:02:28,545 --> 00:02:30,133
Inside a security product.

44
00:02:30,133 --> 00:02:34,629
One of two third-party tools
Bitget ran to protect its systems.

45
00:02:34,679 --> 00:02:37,323
The investigators call them
Product A and Product B.

46
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:39,347
The vendor hasn't been named.

47
00:02:39,397 --> 00:02:41,974
On Product A, a flaw nobody had found yet,

48
00:02:41,974 --> 00:02:45,531
a zero-day, let the attacker
run a hidden script.

49
00:02:45,581 --> 00:02:49,736
The script found a database password,
sitting in plain text.

50
00:02:49,786 --> 00:02:51,777
A spare key taped under the doormat.

51
00:02:51,777 --> 00:02:55,394
Except the doormat was inside
the security office.

52
00:02:55,444 --> 00:02:59,768
From here on, we know someone
is inside. Bitget doesn't.

53
00:02:59,968 --> 00:03:05,343
One caveat. Mandiant dates the attacker's
privileged access only to 24 September.

54
00:03:05,343 --> 00:03:08,847
The two reports haven't been reconciled.

55
00:03:08,897 --> 00:03:11,571
But a hidden script on a guard's
machine can't move money.

56
00:03:11,571 --> 00:03:14,519
To get near the wallets,
they needed something else.

57
00:03:14,569 --> 00:03:17,103
A name. A real one.

58
00:03:17,303 --> 00:03:19,209
Let me stop here for a second.

59
00:03:19,259 --> 00:03:21,969
Companies buy security products
to watch the doors.

60
00:03:21,969 --> 00:03:25,979
They give them deep access,
because that's how they see everything.

61
00:03:26,029 --> 00:03:30,560
Which means if someone takes over the guard,
they don't need to break any doors.

62
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:33,411
They're already standing
in every corridor.

63
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:37,324
The same script had spread.

64
00:03:37,324 --> 00:03:39,418
A second machine on 23 September.

65
00:03:39,418 --> 00:03:42,000
A third, the night of the theft.

66
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:46,563
At 16:07, someone logged
into Product B's management platform.

67
00:03:46,613 --> 00:03:49,794
With the identity
of a real Bitget employee.

68
00:03:49,844 --> 00:03:51,196
That person wasn't there.

69
00:03:51,196 --> 00:03:54,495
Bitget says the credentials
were stolen through the zero-day,

70
00:03:54,495 --> 00:03:58,228
and that it has ruled out,
for now, anyone on the inside.

71
00:03:58,278 --> 00:04:03,167
Mandiant found a web shell on appliance B,
and a channel back to the attacker.

72
00:04:03,217 --> 00:04:07,058
From there, they moved sideways,
to the production wallet job server.

73
00:04:07,058 --> 00:04:09,731
The machine that builds
withdrawal transactions.

74
00:04:09,731 --> 00:04:12,652
And installed malicious packages on it.

75
00:04:12,702 --> 00:04:15,088
Now they were standing next to the money.

76
00:04:15,138 --> 00:04:20,060
But every withdrawal still had to get
past one more thing. Risk control.

77
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:25,346
At 17:49, a tool started
on the wallet host.

78
00:04:25,346 --> 00:04:29,194
SlowMist later recovered
it from deleted files.

79
00:04:29,244 --> 00:04:31,032
It didn't fight risk control.

80
00:04:31,032 --> 00:04:32,939
It forged its answers.

81
00:04:32,989 --> 00:04:34,694
Then it built withdrawal requests,

82
00:04:34,694 --> 00:04:38,152
and pushed them into Bitget's
normal withdrawal process.

83
00:04:38,202 --> 00:04:39,558
According to one analysis,

84
00:04:39,558 --> 00:04:43,812
the system simply took the requester's
word that the checks had passed.

85
00:04:43,862 --> 00:04:45,303
On paper, that was enough.

86
00:04:45,303 --> 00:04:49,479
There was only one way to find
out if it worked in practice.

87
00:04:49,529 --> 00:04:51,108
Try it.

88
00:04:53,309 --> 00:04:56,586
18:31. Two tiny transfers.

89
00:04:56,586 --> 00:05:00,161
93 TRX. Then 0.84 ether.

90
00:05:00,211 --> 00:05:06,105
Tests. Bitget's CEO says they were below
the risk threshold, so no alert went off.

91
00:05:06,155 --> 00:05:08,582
And there was something
odd about these transfers.

92
00:05:08,582 --> 00:05:12,198
Something that could have
given them away. Hold on to it.

93
00:05:12,248 --> 00:05:15,691
Then, nothing. For almost half an hour.

94
00:05:15,741 --> 00:05:21,791
18:58. 34.75 million USDT
leaves in one transfer.

95
00:05:21,841 --> 00:05:23,458
Still no alarm.

96
00:05:23,508 --> 00:05:25,689
So they stopped testing.

97
00:05:26,588 --> 00:05:31,932
19:01. Seven transfers.
Five blockchains. Fifteen seconds.

98
00:05:31,982 --> 00:05:36,037
19:03. One more small test,
from a different wallet.

99
00:05:36,087 --> 00:05:40,820
19:05. Bitget's reconciliation
finally flags the gap.

100
00:05:40,870 --> 00:05:43,115
If you had money on Bitget at that moment,

101
00:05:43,115 --> 00:05:45,575
this is what you saw:
your withdrawal stopped.

102
00:05:45,575 --> 00:05:49,974
Risk control had blocked withdrawals
for every customer on the platform.

103
00:05:50,024 --> 00:05:53,910
19:14. Highest-level emergency.

104
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:55,979
19:16.

105
00:05:56,029 --> 00:06:00,081
Five transfers. Five chains. Nine seconds.

106
00:06:00,131 --> 00:06:03,240
About $203 million.

107
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:06,553
The customers had been locked
out 11 minutes earlier.

108
00:06:06,603 --> 00:06:08,572
The attacker hadn't.

109
00:06:10,672 --> 00:06:13,474
Blocking customer withdrawals
was the wrong switch.

110
00:06:13,474 --> 00:06:16,717
The forged requests weren't
customer withdrawals.

111
00:06:16,767 --> 00:06:18,188
According to one analysis,

112
00:06:18,188 --> 00:06:21,906
on the warm wallets nothing stood
between a request and a signature.

113
00:06:21,906 --> 00:06:22,836
No second person.

114
00:06:22,836 --> 00:06:25,235
No list of allowed destinations.

115
00:06:25,285 --> 00:06:27,481
So the signing service kept running.

116
00:06:27,481 --> 00:06:31,469
For 2 hours and 39 minutes
after the first alert.

117
00:06:32,970 --> 00:06:35,899
Two separate estimates
put the money that left

118
00:06:35,899 --> 00:06:38,319
after the alarm at about $238 million.

119
00:06:38,319 --> 00:06:40,751
More than half of the theft.

120
00:06:40,801 --> 00:06:44,394
At 20:40, Bitget started
moving funds into cold storage.

121
00:06:44,394 --> 00:06:47,703
Still not sure whether
keys had been taken.

122
00:06:47,753 --> 00:06:51,455
The last traced transfer left at 21:23.

123
00:06:51,505 --> 00:06:55,875
Then the attacker tried to rewrite
withdrawal records directly in the database.

124
00:06:55,875 --> 00:06:57,811
Covering tracks, or going for more.

125
00:06:57,811 --> 00:06:59,028
The reports don't say.

126
00:06:59,028 --> 00:07:01,791
Two fake Bitcoin orders returned errors.

127
00:07:01,841 --> 00:07:07,920
At 21:44, Bitget shut down
the withdrawal services, signing included.

128
00:07:07,970 --> 00:07:08,785
The next morning,

129
00:07:08,785 --> 00:07:12,792
Bitget found the root cause
and reported the theft to law enforcement.

130
00:07:12,842 --> 00:07:16,248
And when investigators went back
to those first test transfers,

131
00:07:16,248 --> 00:07:18,924
the odd detail was right there.

132
00:07:20,624 --> 00:07:23,838
Every Ethereum transfer
sets a fuel limit. Gas.

133
00:07:23,838 --> 00:07:27,875
Bitget's normal customer
payouts used about 63,000.

134
00:07:27,925 --> 00:07:33,224
The attacker's transfers used
exactly 100,000, or 200,000.

135
00:07:33,274 --> 00:07:38,509
In the 8 days before, those
wallets sent 35,678 transactions.

136
00:07:38,509 --> 00:07:42,190
Not one customer payout did that.

137
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:43,964
On Zcash, it was even louder.

138
00:07:43,964 --> 00:07:47,770
Each stolen transfer pulled
together about 1,000 pieces of coin.

139
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:51,909
The wallet's biggest transaction
before that had used 54.

140
00:07:51,959 --> 00:07:55,243
The forged commands were deleted
after each transfer.

141
00:07:55,293 --> 00:07:56,564
The fingerprints weren't.

142
00:07:56,564 --> 00:07:58,988
Nobody was watching for them.

143
00:07:59,038 --> 00:08:01,716
Now the thief had $388 million.

144
00:08:01,716 --> 00:08:05,653
And a new problem: how to keep it.

145
00:08:07,353 --> 00:08:11,138
The stablecoins went first,
because stablecoins can be frozen.

146
00:08:11,138 --> 00:08:14,925
All the USDT was swapped
for ether within 20 minutes.

147
00:08:14,975 --> 00:08:17,320
The XRP went to Bitcoin through THORChain.

148
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:22,325
Part of the Zcash disappeared into a
private pool, where it can't be followed.

149
00:08:22,375 --> 00:08:25,124
Bitget asked THORChain
to block the addresses.

150
00:08:25,124 --> 00:08:28,841
THORChain declined, citing
its permissionless design.

151
00:08:28,891 --> 00:08:33,159
In the end, less than 0.3% was frozen.

152
00:08:33,209 --> 00:08:36,250
In November, the Bitcoin
started moving again.

153
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:40,151
About $161 million in 11 days,
most of it through CoinJoin,

154
00:08:40,151 --> 00:08:44,505
which mixes many people's
coins into one transaction.

155
00:08:44,555 --> 00:08:47,546
And the people moving it
weren't hiding very well.

156
00:08:47,596 --> 00:08:50,672
ZachXBT says the launderers
asked for customer support

157
00:08:50,672 --> 00:08:53,633
in the public chats
of the services they were using.

158
00:08:53,633 --> 00:08:55,893
Failed swaps. Missing payouts.

159
00:08:55,943 --> 00:08:58,774
He says one of them had
handled stolen money before.

160
00:08:58,774 --> 00:09:01,680
From the Kelp DAO exploit in April.

161
00:09:03,379 --> 00:09:04,954
So who was behind it?

162
00:09:05,004 --> 00:09:08,173
On the night of the hack,
Bitget's CEO, Gracy Chen,

163
00:09:08,173 --> 00:09:13,710
said some IP addresses matched VPN
services used by a North Korean group.

164
00:09:16,609 --> 00:09:18,917
Four days later, she was more careful.

165
00:09:18,917 --> 00:09:22,626
That, she said, was based
on preliminary indicators.

166
00:09:22,676 --> 00:09:27,952
The analysts at Chainalysis, Elliptic
and TRM Labs all lean the same way.

167
00:09:28,002 --> 00:09:32,863
Then, 5 weeks later, Binance
lost more than $6 billion.

168
00:09:32,913 --> 00:09:35,286
And Chainalysis watched
the stolen Binance money

169
00:09:35,286 --> 00:09:37,813
meet the Bitget money on the blockchain.

170
00:09:37,863 --> 00:09:42,473
ZachXBT says both arrived, through
THORChain, at the same address.

171
00:09:42,523 --> 00:09:44,517
Two days after the Binance theft,

172
00:09:44,517 --> 00:09:48,690
the FBI named the thieves:
North Korea's TraderTraitor.

173
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:51,202
For Bitget, no government has said a word.

174
00:09:51,202 --> 00:09:54,314
And Mandiant's report names no one at all.

175
00:09:54,364 --> 00:09:56,919
Which brings us back to that loan.

176
00:09:57,119 --> 00:10:03,143
In February 2025, when Bybit was robbed,
Bitget sent 40,000 ether the same day.

177
00:10:03,193 --> 00:10:05,302
No interest. No collateral.

178
00:10:05,352 --> 00:10:08,315
The FBI blamed that theft on North Korea.

179
00:10:08,365 --> 00:10:11,643
Nineteen months later,
the money was leaving Bitget.

180
00:10:11,693 --> 00:10:13,420
No government has named anyone.

181
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:16,426
But the analysts point the same way.

182
00:10:18,126 --> 00:10:20,395
If you had money on Bitget,
you didn't lose it.

183
00:10:20,395 --> 00:10:23,891
Bitget says it covered the loss
from its own protection fund.

184
00:10:23,941 --> 00:10:27,129
Every withdrawal was back by 2 October.

185
00:10:27,179 --> 00:10:30,274
The fund has been rebuilt
to just over $300 million.

186
00:10:30,274 --> 00:10:32,394
Still less than before.

187
00:10:32,444 --> 00:10:33,875
The vulnerability is fixed.

188
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:37,181
Critical operations now
need more than one approval.

189
00:10:37,231 --> 00:10:40,897
And Bitget says it is reviewing
how it picks its security vendors.

190
00:10:40,897 --> 00:10:43,841
The vendor still hasn't been named.

191
00:10:44,041 --> 00:10:48,876
It started with a transfer of 93 TRX,
at half past two in the morning.

192
00:10:48,876 --> 00:10:50,907
Nobody looked at it.

193
00:10:50,957 --> 00:10:52,863
Bitget bought tools to guard the door.

194
00:10:52,863 --> 00:10:56,371
For weeks, someone else
may have been holding them.

195
00:10:56,421 --> 00:11:00,072
And when the alarm finally rang,
the exchange locked out its customers,

196
00:11:00,072 --> 00:11:02,925
and left the signing machine running.

197
00:11:02,975 --> 00:11:06,386
I'm Thomas Archer. This was Siphon Files.

